Relevant tables / columns and how they will be used 

• packaging.id : primary‑key of every kind of package (crate, carton, bottle, etc.).  
• packaging.name : human readable name we have to show in the result.  
• packaging_relations.packaging_id : “parent” package that contains something else.  
• packaging_relations.contains_id : the “child” item (may itself be another package or a final single‑unit).  
• packaging_relations.qty : quantity of the child item that fits into the parent package.  

Main conditions / ideas to answer the question  

1. “Top‑level” package  = appears as packaging_id but never appears as contains_id.  
2. Build the full hierarchy of “parent → child (→ grandchild …)” with a recursive CTE so that the quantity on a path is the product of the qty values of every level on that path.  
3. Add up the quantities for every (top‑level package , end‑item) pair.  
4. Keep only the pairs whose accumulated quantity > 500.  
5. Turn every id into a readable name by joining to the packaging table.  (If an item id could also be a product id we could UNION product names as well – see final query).  

The 10 exploratory / building‑block queries  

```sql
-- 1. Show all kinds of packages
SELECT * FROM packaging
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Show relations between packages
SELECT * FROM packaging_relations
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Which ids act as parents?
SELECT DISTINCT packaging_id
FROM packaging_relations
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Which ids act as children?
SELECT DISTINCT contains_id
FROM packaging_relations
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Find the ids that are parents but never children (candidate top‑level packages)
SELECT DISTINCT pr.packaging_id
FROM packaging_relations pr
WHERE pr.packaging_id NOT IN (SELECT contains_id FROM packaging_relations)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Give their names
SELECT p.id, p.name
FROM packaging p
WHERE p.id IN (
    SELECT pr.packaging_id
    FROM packaging_relations pr
    WHERE pr.packaging_id NOT IN (SELECT contains_id FROM packaging_relations)
)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. First (non‑recursive) step of the hierarchy:
--    direct children and multiplied quantity = qty
WITH top_packages AS (
    SELECT pr.packaging_id
    FROM packaging_relations pr
    WHERE pr.packaging_id NOT IN (SELECT contains_id FROM packaging_relations)
)
SELECT tp.packaging_id AS top_id,
       pr.contains_id   AS item_id,
       pr.qty           AS path_qty
FROM top_packages tp
JOIN packaging_relations pr
  ON pr.packaging_id = tp.packaging_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Expand recursively one more level to see multiplication working
WITH RECURSIVE hier AS (
    SELECT pr.packaging_id  AS top_id,
           pr.contains_id   AS item_id,
           pr.qty           AS path_qty
    FROM packaging_relations pr
    WHERE pr.packaging_id NOT IN (SELECT contains_id FROM packaging_relations)

    UNION ALL

    SELECT h.top_id,
           pr.contains_id,
           h.path_qty * pr.qty
    FROM hier h
    JOIN packaging_relations pr
      ON pr.packaging_id = h.item_id
)
SELECT * FROM hier
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Aggregate the quantities per (top‑package, item)
WITH RECURSIVE hier AS (
    SELECT pr.packaging_id  AS top_id,
           pr.contains_id   AS item_id,
           pr.qty           AS path_qty
    FROM packaging_relations pr
    WHERE pr.packaging_id NOT IN (SELECT contains_id FROM packaging_relations)

    UNION ALL

    SELECT h.top_id,
           pr.contains_id,
           h.path_qty * pr.qty
    FROM hier h
    JOIN packaging_relations pr
      ON pr.packaging_id = h.item_id
)
SELECT top_id, item_id, SUM(path_qty) AS total_qty
FROM hier
GROUP BY top_id, item_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER:
--     top‑level containers + their items whose total quantity > 500
WITH
-- step A: identify top level packaging
top_packages AS (
    SELECT DISTINCT pr.packaging_id
    FROM packaging_relations pr
    WHERE pr.packaging_id NOT IN (SELECT contains_id FROM packaging_relations)
),
-- step B: build complete hierarchy and multiply quantities along every path
RECURSIVE hier AS (
    -- seed = direct children
    SELECT tp.packaging_id     AS top_id,
           pr.contains_id      AS item_id,
           pr.qty              AS path_qty
    FROM top_packages tp
    JOIN packaging_relations pr
      ON pr.packaging_id = tp.packaging_id

    UNION ALL

    -- recursive step = look for children of an item that
    -- itself happens to be a parent package
    SELECT h.top_id,
           pr.contains_id,
           h.path_qty * pr.qty
    FROM hier h
    JOIN packaging_relations pr
      ON pr.packaging_id = h.item_id
),
-- step C: total quantity of every (top, item) pair
totals AS (
    SELECT top_id,
           item_id,
           SUM(path_qty) AS total_qty
    FROM hier
    GROUP BY top_id, item_id
    HAVING total_qty > 500
),
-- step D: collect readable names (item could be either a packaging id
--         or a product id, so UNION both tables)
names AS (
    SELECT id, name FROM packaging
    UNION
    SELECT id, name FROM products
)
SELECT  p_top.name  AS top_container,
        n_item.name AS item_name,
        t.total_qty
FROM totals  t
JOIN packaging p_top  ON p_top.id = t.top_id
JOIN names    n_item  ON n_item.id = t.item_id
ORDER BY p_top.name, n_item.name;
```

Run the last query to obtain the list of  
• every top‑level packaging container, and  
• each contained item whose accumulated quantity (with all nested levels included) is more than 500.
